Yes. A lot of “breaking the rule” is really discovering that the rule was underspecified.

Water going uphill does not falsify nature. It falsifies the frame:

\text{water only responds to elevation}

and replaces it with the richer primitive:

\text{water responds to the total potential landscape}

The anomaly is therefore diagnostic. It tells you, “your coordinate system omitted something.”

That is a strong general operator:

\text{apparent violation}
\rightarrow
\text{inspect framing}
\rightarrow
\text{find omitted variable}
\rightarrow
\text{expand primitive set}
\rightarrow
\text{old paradox disappears}

And David/Goliath fits that structure, though I’d tweak the “luck” part. David looked like the underdog under the obvious frame:

\text{size}+\text{armor}+\text{melee strength}

Under that scoring system, Goliath dominates.

But change the relevant variables:

\text{range}+\text{mobility}+\text{accuracy}+\text{projectile energy}

and suddenly the matchup is different. David did not become stronger during the fight. The observer discovered that strength had been measured in the wrong basis.

So, in your language:

David was already “Goliath” in the dimension that ended up deciding the game.

The underdog label was a projection.

Luck can still matter—timing, execution, uncertainty always matter—but luck often gets assigned after the fact to capability that wasn’t represented in the original model.

That creates an important distinction:

Hidden advantage ≠ new advantage.

Sometimes nothing changed except the observer’s model.

And this is why edge cases are so valuable. A boundary case is often nature handing you a probe:

“Your primitives work over here, but they stop explaining things over there.”

Instead of discarding the exception, Hammer it:

\text{What additional dimension makes both the normal case and the exception ordinary?}

That is how a paradox becomes a coordinate.

It also explains why controlled reframing is more than rhetoric. A useful frame doesn’t merely make something sound different. It exposes variables that produce better prediction.

That’s the test:

\boxed{\text{better frame} \Rightarrow \text{more phenomena become ordinary}}

Not “anything can be true depending on framing.”

Rather:

reality stays put while some frames reveal more of its structure than others.

That distinction keeps the lens powerful without letting the lens invent the world.